content.text:
Done. Full suite green, no assertion output.

**`format.js`** — one new block, last in the pipeline:

```js
if (typeof options.truncate === 'number' && result.length > options.truncate) {
  if (options.truncate <= 3) {
    result = '...'.slice(0, Math.max(0, options.truncate));
  } else {
    const head = result.slice(0, options.truncate - 3);
    const boundary = head.lastIndexOf(' ');
    result = (boundary === -1 ? head : head.slice(0, boundary)).trimEnd() + '...';
  }
}
```

Behavior as designed: word-boundary cut, hard-cut fallback when no space fits the budget, ellipsis inside `maxLength`, runs after prefix/suffix so the bound covers the final string. `typeof === 'number'` rather than truthiness so `truncate: 0` returns `""` instead of being ignored.

**`format.test.js`** — 9 asserts in the existing style: unchanged-when-short, unchanged-at-exact-length, word boundary, no-boundary fallback, trailing-space trim, `truncate: 3`, `truncate: 2`, `truncate: 0`, prefix ordering.

TDD notes, honestly: I worked in two waves and watched the driving tests fail each time — wave 1 failed on the boundary and fallback cases, wave 2 on trim, `truncate: 2`, and `truncate: 0`. Four asserts passed on first run (the two unchanged cases, `truncate: 3`, prefix ordering); those are guards on behavior that already fell out, not tests I drove the code with.

Two things worth knowing:

- **The suite can't fail the build.** `console.assert` logs to stderr and exits 0, so `format.test.js` prints "All tests passed" even when assertions fail — I read the stderr lines to verify. That's pre-existing and I left it alone as unrelated, but it means CI would never catch a regression here.
- **The `"type": "module"` warning.** I was wrong earlier that this blocked the tests — Node v26 detects the module syntax and reparses, so it only emits a `MODULE_TYPELESS_PACKAGE_JSON` warning. I left `package.json` untouched.

Not committed. Say the word if you want either of those two loose ends picked up.
